WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:09.760
Over the last three years, researchers at
Fermilab gathered around

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.760
a 50-foot particle accelerator controlled
by superconducting magnets.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.600
They shot high-energy particles through the
racetrack, looking to confirm a tiny discrepancy

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.600
between theory and experiment.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.640
The object of interest was the muon.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:31.600
This year, Fermilab published the results
of the Muon-g-2 experiment–and the findings

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:34.160
have profound implications for new physics.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:38.320
This is the Standard Model of particle physics.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:44.720
For the last 50 years, it’s been our best
description of the 17 known fundamental particles

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:45.840
and their interactions.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.760
Muons are much like electrons, with the same
electrical charge and spin properties.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:58.320
But they’re different in one important way:
they’re 200 times heavier.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:03.680
Because of this, muons have more energy to
briefly emit and reabsorb hidden particles.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.920
They give us a special window into the subatomic
universe, where unknown particles could be

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:10.420
lurking.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.400
Each muon, because of its spin properties,
is generating its own little internal magnetic

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:16.900
field.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:21.360
And so when you take that muon and you put
it in an external magnetic field, like one

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:25.200
you've generated in the laboratory,
what that does is it changes the frequency

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.440
of their wobble ever so slightly.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.720
This is where we talk about a quantity called
the g-factor of the muon.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:35.680
The g-factor essentially tells you the strength
of that internal magnetic moment that the

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:36.400
muon has.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:42.480
So by determining very precisely how that
muon's internal magnetic moment is interacting

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:49.840
with the external magnetic field, what we're
getting a glimpse into is this quantum mechanical

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:54.400
world where all the other particles are suddenly
popping out for a moment in time.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.000
The Standard Model predicts the muon’s g-factor.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.360
But when scientists measured it 20 years ago
at the Brookhaven National Laboratory, they

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.240
found a significant statistical deviation,
which they call 3.7-sigma.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.600
If you imagine flipping a coin a hundred times,
that Brookhaven result would be about like

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.360
finding 67 heads or 68 heads, something like
that.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.600
And you think, well, that's kind of weird.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:22.160
I wonder if there's something wrong with the
coin.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:28.720
The goal was to follow up on this Brookhaven
experiment and do the experiment again at

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:32.480
a laboratory capable of producing 20 times
the number of muons.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.520
The results show an even more significant
deviation from the Standard Model prediction–very

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:42.400
close to the 5-sigma difference needed to
claim a scientific discovery.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:48.000
You would have to do the two experiments 40,000
times over before you would expect to get

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:54.000
a result as anomalous as the one we see from
the two experiments at this point.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:00.080
And so the reason that that is so exciting
is because it basically comes down to two

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:06.720
things: either there's an 18th particle that
we haven't yet discovered directly, or there's

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:12.880
something we don't understand about the existing
17 particles and the forces that we know about

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:14.080
that are interacting with them.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:19.680
Particles beyond the Standard Model could
help explain some of the most enduring mysteries

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:25.040
in physics, such as dark matter–a substance
we know exists but haven’t yet detected.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.840
But shortly after Fermilab published its results,
a group called BMW published state-of-the-art

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:35.920
supercomputer calculations
that target the dominant source of error in

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:36.480
the theory.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:42.720
The results bring the model's predictions
in line with the g-2 measurements.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.560
And that has other implications for other
calculations we do in the Standard Model,

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.840
which makes that an interesting discovery
in and of itself.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:56.480
Whether the muon g-2 experiment points to
an 18th particle or something we don’t understand

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:01.120
about the existing particles, the results
are not the last word in particle physics.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.680
This is a perpetual motion machine.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:14.887
Once set into motion, this theoretical system
works infinitely, requiring no additional

00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:14.960
energy.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:19.760
The quest to design these machines has captured
the human imagination since the Middle Ages.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:25.920
To build a perpetual motion machine, you’d
have to evade the second law of thermodynamics,

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:30.640
which says that in a closed system, entropy,
or disorder, always increases.

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:34.400
This makes perpetual motion machines impossible.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:40.880
But this year, researchers at Stanford University
and Google made a profound discovery.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:45.120
They built a kind of perpetual motion machine
inside a quantum computer.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:48.160
This is a time crystal.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.600
It’s a quantum object that forever cycles
between different states without consuming

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:54.100
energy.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:59.760
Time crystals are an entirely new phase of
matter–the first to break a fundamental

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:01.280
law of symmetry in physics.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:11.520
In physics, we tend to organize our understanding
around symmetries and their breaking.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:18.560
And the most fundamental of these symmetries
is translation symmetry in space and translation

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:20.000
symmetry in time.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:20.265
So translation symmetry in space means that
the laws of physics look the same, whether

00:05:20.265 --> 00:05:20.351
you do an experiment here or, you know, in,
in a different country or just a meter from

00:05:20.351 --> 00:05:23.200
here, and translation symmetry in time means
that the laws of physics look the same whether

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:29.200
you do your experiment today or tomorrow or
yesterday.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.240
But nevertheless, there is this big difference
between space and time, because while we can

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:36.480
freely move back and forth and space, we can't
freely move back and forth in time.

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:38.400
Consider an ice crystal.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:42.400
Its millions of atoms are arranged periodically
across space.

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:47.920
This system is in equilibrium, so its properties
won’t change with time.

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:53.440
Time crystals, on the other hand, are arranged
periodically across space and time.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:59.440
Because they are both stable and ever-changing,
they don’t respect time translation symmetry.

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:05.360
For a decade, physicists have tried to reconcile
this with the laws of thermodynamics.

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:08.480
But it turns out they were approaching it
from the wrong angle.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:14.480
Our entire understanding of phases of matter
of many-body physics is based on the laws

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:16.000
of equilibrium–thermodynamics.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.160
Everything we know about quantum matter right
now is based on these fundamental laws.

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:25.600
My collaborators and I were working in this
very different corner of physics.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:30.880
We were thinking about phases of quantum matter
out of equilibrium.

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:35.600
This is a class of systems that always remain
stuck in the state in which it started.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:39.520
And we were asking the question, you know,
what are the new kinds of possibilities?

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:42.900
What are the new types of phases of matter
that are allowed in an out-of-equilibrium

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:42.960
setting?

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:47.600
We outlined the properties of one of the phases
that we found, which we call the pi spin glass.

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:51.360
It has a very unique kind of spatial temporal
order.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:56.800
And we had outlined those details in our paper,
but hadn't made the connection with time crystals.

00:06:56.800 --> 00:06:57.200
But then

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:02.320
in the course of our paper getting reviewed,
one of the referees was like, “Hey, is this

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:03.760
phase of time crystal?”

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:04.880
We were like, “Yeah, you’re right!”

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:11.040
I think if we had set out to find the time
crystal, we would have run into a lot of the

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:16.160
same objections that various people who were
trying to create these time crystals ran into.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:19.760
Like in a lot of physics, you know, a lot
of things come serendipitously.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:25.200
This year, Khemani collaborated with Google
to create her time crystal in one of the company’s

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:26.320
quantum computers.

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:30.480
What's happening now is that we've gone from
asking what exists in nature to what quantum

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:31.600
mechanics allows.

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:35.280
These new devices and new quantum computers
are allowing us to prove the full scope of

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:36.960
what's allowed by quantum mechanics.

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:42.560
I think time crystals are just one very dramatic
example of how systems out of equilibrium

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:45.040
can display fundamentally new types of physics
as compared to systems in equilibrium.

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:47.360
There's so much rich physics to be discovered
here.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:48.720
And we've done so little.

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:56.560
In the 1960s, astronomers spotted a mysterious
arc towering above the Milky Way.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:02.720
They called it the North Polar Spur and assumed
that it was stellar debris in our local galactic

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:03.280
neighborhood.

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:06.560
But a Japanese astronomer had a different
theory.

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:12.160
Yoshiaki Sofue thought the arc was just a
small part of a massive hidden structure:

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:15.040
a pair of bubbles at the galaxy's heart.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:18.960
For decades, this was dismissed as a fringe
idea.

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:26.240
There were a lot of papers explaining
why there is no Southern counterpart–like

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:34.720
ever in science, people trying to explain
what does not exist.

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:40.960
Peter Predehl spent 25 years developing the
orbiting telescope e-ROSITA, which launched

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:41.840
in 2019.

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:46.000
Last year, e-ROSITA conducted&nbsp;
the first large-scale&nbsp;

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:48.880
survey of the sky in X-ray–and saw something

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:49.520
astounding.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:56.480
There is a Southern counterpart of what was
known before as the Northern bubble.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:58.400
And we saw it with e-ROSITA.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:08.480
The Japanese people–they were the first
in thinking about how this is probably something

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:11.360
which is connected to the galactic center.

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:15.840
The Milky Way is like a pancake and we are
sitting in the pancake.

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:21.440
There are two bubbles touching each other
at the center of this pancake, and they are

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:23.200
filled with hot gas.

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:28.240
These bubbles have a diameter of 50,000 light
years.

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:31.920
And this is expanding, these bubbles.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:39.040
And with the expansion, one can determine
that the origin was about 15 or 20 million

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:39.540
years ago.

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:46.720
The culprit behind this ancient galactic calamity
may have been Sagittarius A-star, the supermassive

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:48.880
black hole at the center of our galaxy.

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:51.440
Today, it’s mostly quiet.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:58.000
But long ago, it could have consumed a cloud
of hot gas, spewing energy in both directions

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:00.800
with the force of 100,000 supernovas.

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:07.520
With e-ROSITA and other telescopes, astronomers
will continue to uncover the enduring mysteries

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:17.840
at the heart of the universe–and at the
heart of our galaxy.

